+package followed by a \(lq.test\(rq suffix, as in
+\(lqmath/rand.test\(rq.
+When building a test, it is sometimes necessary to rebuild certain
+dependencies specially for that test (most commonly the tested package
+itself).
+The reported import path of a package recompiled for a particular test
+binary is followed by a space and the name of the test binary in
+brackets, as in \(lqmath/rand [math/rand.test]\(rq or \(lqregexp
+[sort.test]\(rq.
+The ForTest field is also set to the name of the package being tested
+(\(lqmath/rand\(rq or \(lqsort\(rq in the previous examples).
+.PP
+The Dir, Target, Shlib, Root, ConflictDir, and Export file paths are all
+absolute paths.
+.PP
+By default, the lists GoFiles, CgoFiles, and so on hold names of files
+in Dir (that is, paths relative to Dir, not absolute paths).
+The generated files added when using the \f[B]\-compiled\f[R] and
+\f[B]\-test\f[R] flags are absolute paths referring to cached copies of
+generated Go source files.
+Although they are Go source files, the paths may not end in \(lq.go\(rq.
+.PP
+The \f[B]\-m\f[R] flag causes list to list modules instead of packages.
+.PP
+When listing modules, the \f[B]\-f\f[R] flag still specifies a format
+template applied to a Go struct, but now a Module struct:
+.IP
+.EX
+type Module struct {
+    Path       string        // module path
+    Query      string        // version query corresponding to this version
+    Version    string        // module version
+    Versions   []string      // available module versions
+    Replace    *Module       // replaced by this module
+    Time       *time.Time    // time version was created
+    Update     *Module       // available update (with \-u)
+    Main       bool          // is this the main module?
+    Indirect   bool          // module is only indirectly needed by main module
+    Dir        string        // directory holding local copy of files, if any
+    GoMod      string        // path to go.mod file describing module, if any
+    GoVersion  string        // go version used in module
+    Retracted  []string      // retraction information, if any (with \-retracted or \-u)
+    Deprecated string        // deprecation message, if any (with \-u)
+    Error      *ModuleError  // error loading module
+    Sum        string        // checksum for path, version (as in go.sum)
+    GoModSum   string        // checksum for go.mod (as in go.sum)
+    Origin     any           // provenance of module
+    Reuse      bool          // reuse of old module info is safe
+}
+
+type ModuleError struct {
+    Err string // the error itself
+}
+.EE
+.PP
+The file GoMod refers to may be outside the module directory if the
+module is in the module cache or if the \f[B]\-modfile\f[R] flag is
+used.
+.PP
+The default output is to print the module path and then information
+about the version and replacement if any.
+For example, `go list \f[B]\-m\f[R] all' might print:
+.IP
+.EX
+my/main/module
+golang.org/x/text v0.3.0 => /tmp/text
+rsc.io/pdf v0.1.1
+.EE
+.PP
+The Module struct has a String method that formats this line of output,
+so that the default format is equivalent to \f[B]\-f\f[R] `{{.String}}'.
+.PP
+Note that when a module has been replaced, its Replace field describes
+the replacement module, and its Dir field is set to the replacement\(cqs
+source code, if present.
+(That is, if Replace is non\-nil, then Dir is set to Replace.Dir, with
+no access to the replaced source code.)
+.PP
+The \f[B]\-u\f[R] flag adds information about available upgrades.
+When the latest version of a given module is newer than the current one,
+list \f[B]\-u\f[R] sets the Module\(cqs Update field to information
+about the newer module.
+list \f[B]\-u\f[R] will also set the module\(cqs Retracted field if the
+current version is retracted.
+The Module\(cqs String method indicates an available upgrade by
+formatting the newer version in brackets after the current version.
+If a version is retracted, the string \(lq(retracted)\(rq will follow
+it.
+For example, `go list \f[B]\-m\f[R] \f[B]\-u\f[R] all' might print:
+.IP
+.EX
+my/main/module
+golang.org/x/text v0.3.0 [v0.4.0] => /tmp/text
+rsc.io/pdf v0.1.1 (retracted) [v0.1.2]
+.EE
+.PP
+(For tools, `go list \f[B]\-m\f[R] \f[B]\-u\f[R] \f[B]\-json\f[R] all'
+may be more convenient to parse.)
+.PP
+The \f[B]\-versions\f[R] flag causes list to set the Module\(cqs
+Versions field to a list of all known versions of that module, ordered
+according to semantic versioning, earliest to latest.
+The flag also changes the default output format to display the module
+path followed by the space\-separated version list.
+.PP
+The \f[B]\-retracted\f[R] flag causes list to report information about
+retracted module versions.
+When \f[B]\-retracted\f[R] is used with \f[B]\-f\f[R] or
+\f[B]\-json\f[R], the Retracted field explains why the version was
+retracted.
+The strings are taken from comments on the retract directive in the
+module\(cqs go.mod file.
+When \f[B]\-retracted\f[R] is used with \f[B]\-versions\f[R], retracted
+versions are listed together with unretracted versions.
+The \f[B]\-retracted\f[R] flag may be used with or without
+\f[B]\-m\f[R].
+.PP
+The arguments to list \f[B]\-m\f[R] are interpreted as a list of
+modules, not packages.
+The main module is the module containing the current directory.
+The active modules are the main module and its dependencies.
+With no arguments, list \f[B]\-m\f[R] shows the main module.
+With arguments, list \f[B]\-m\f[R] shows the modules specified by the
+arguments.
+Any of the active modules can be specified by its module path.
+The special pattern \(lqall\(rq specifies all the active modules, first
+the main module and then dependencies sorted by module path.
+A pattern containing \(lq\&...\(rq specifies the active modules whose
+module paths match the pattern.
+A query of the form path\(atversion specifies the result of that query,
+which is not limited to active modules.
+See `go help modules' for more about module queries.
+.PP
+The template function \(lqmodule\(rq takes a single string argument that
+must be a module path or query and returns the specified module as a
+Module struct.
+If an error occurs, the result will be a Module struct with a non\-nil
+Error field.
+.PP
+When using \f[B]\-m\f[R], the \f[B]\-reuse\f[R]=\f[I]old\f[R].json flag
+accepts the name of file containing the JSON output of a previous `go
+list \f[B]\-m\f[R] \f[B]\-json\f[R]' invocation with the same set of
+modifier flags (such as \f[B]\-u\f[R], \f[B]\-retracted\f[R], and
+\f[B]\-versions\f[R]).
+The go command may use this file to determine that a module is unchanged
+since the previous invocation and avoid redownloading information about
+it.
+Modules that are not redownloaded will be marked in the new output by
+setting the Reuse field to true.
+Normally the module cache provides this kind of reuse automatically; the
+\f[B]\-reuse\f[R] flag can be useful on systems that do not preserve the
+module cache.
+.PP
+For more about build flags, see `go help build'.
+.PP
+For more about specifying packages, see `go help packages'.
+.PP
+For more about modules, see https://go.dev/ref/mod.
