+.\" Automatically generated by Pandoc 3.10.2
+.\"
+.TH "GO\-PACKAGES" "7" "August 2026" "Go 1.27" "Go programming tools and commands"
+.SH NAME
+packages \- package lists and patterns
+.SH DESCRIPTION
+Many commands apply to a set of packages:
+.PP
+\f[B]go\f[R] <\f[I]action\f[R]> [\f[I]packages\f[R]]
+.PP
+Usually, [\f[I]packages\f[R]] is a list of package patterns, which can
+take several forms:
+.IP \(bu 2
+A relative or absolute path to a file system directory, which can
+contain \(lq\&...\(rq wildcard elements.
+.IP \(bu 2
+An import path, which can also contain \(lq\&...\(rq wildcard elements.
+.IP \(bu 2
+A reserved name that expands to a set of packages
+.IP \(bu 2
+A list of files
+.PP
+If no import paths are given, the action applies to the package in the
+current directory.
+.PP
+\(lq\&...\(rq elements in filesystem or import paths expand to match 0
+or more path elements.
+Specific rules are described below.
+.PP
+File system patterns
+.PP
+Patterns beginning with a file system root like / on Unixes, or a volume
+name like C: on Windows are interpreted as absolute file system paths.
+Patterns beginning with a \(lq.\(rq or \(lq..\(rq element are
+interpreted as relative file system paths.
+File system paths denote the package contained within the given
+directory.
+.PP
+Relative paths can be used as a shorthand on the command line.
+If you are working in the directory containing the code imported as
+\(lqunicode\(rq and want to run the tests for \(lqunicode/utf8\(rq, you
+can type \(lqgo test ./utf8\(rq instead of needing to specify the full
+path.
+Similarly, in the reverse situation, \(lqgo test ..\(rq will test
+\(lqunicode\(rq from the \(lqunicode/utf8\(rq directory.
+Relative patterns are also allowed, such as \(lqgo test ./\&...\(rq to
+test all subdirectories.
+.PP
+File system patterns expanded with the \(lq\&...\(rq wildcard exclude
+the following:
+.IP \(bu 2
+Directories named \(lqvendor\(rq
+.IP \(bu 2
+Directories named \(lqtestdata\(rq
+.IP \(bu 2
+Files and directories with names beginning with \(lq_\(rq or \(lq.\(rq
+.IP \(bu 2
+Directories that contain a go.mod file
+.IP \(bu 2
+Directories matching an ignore directive in a module\(cqs go.mod file
+.PP
+These can be included by either using them in the prefix, or changing
+into the directories.
+For example, \(lq./\&...\(rq won\(cqt match a \(lq./testdata/foo\(rq
+package, but \(lq./testdata/\&...\(rq will.
+.PP
+Directories containing other go modules, which are denoted by the
+presence of a go.mod file, can only be matched by changing the working
+directory into module.
+.PP
+Import path patterns
+.PP
+Patterns may be import paths as described in \(lqgo help importpath\(rq.
+Import path patterns natch the packages from modules in the build list.
+The \(lqbuild list\(rq is the list of module versions used for a build.
+See https://go.dev/ref/mod#glos\-build\-list for more details.
+.PP
+Some commands accept versioned package patterns, such as:
+.PD 0
+.P
+.PD
+\(lqexample.com/my/module\(atv1.2.3\(rq
+.PD 0
+.P
+.PD
+These describe the matching package at the given version, independent of
+the versions used by the current module.
+.PP
+Import path patterns may also use a \(lq\&...\(rq wildcard, such as:
+.PD 0
+.P
+.PD
+\(lqexample.com/my/module/\&...\(rq.
+.PD 0
+.P
+.PD
+This can be combined with the version specifier such as:
+.PD 0
+.P
+.PD
+\(lqexample.com/my/module/\&...\(atlatest\(rq.
+.PP
+Import path pattern expansion with \(lq\&...\(rq depends on context:
+.IP \(bu 2
+\(lqprefix/\&...\(rq matches all packages in modules in the build list
+that share the prefix, even if they belong to different modules.
+.IP \(bu 2
+patterns that include a version specifier such as in
+\(lqprefix/\&...\(atlatest\(rq only match packages from the module that
+\(lqprefix\(rq belongs to.
+.PP
+Reserved names
+.PP
+The following reserved names expand to a set of packages:
+.IP \(bu 2
+\(lqwork\(rq expands to all packages in the main module (or workspace
+modules).
+.IP \(bu 2
+\(lqtool\(rq expands to the tools defined in the current module\(cqs
+go.mod file.
+.IP \(bu 2
+\(lqall\(rq expands to all packages in the main module (or workspace
+modules) and their dependencies, including dependencies needed by tests
+of any of those.
+In the legacy GOPATH mode, \(lqall\(rq expands to all packages found in
+all the GOPATH trees.
+.IP \(bu 2
+\(lqstd\(rq expands to all the packages in the standard library and
+their internal libraries.
+.IP \(bu 2
+\(lqcmd\(rq expands to the Go repository\(cqs commands and their
+internal libraries.
+.PP
+List of .go files
+.PP
+If the pattern is a list of Go files rather than a complete package, the
+go command synthesizes a virtual package named
+\(lqcommand\-line\-arguments\(rq containing just the given files.
+In most cases, it is an error to do so (e.g.\ \(lqgo build main.go\(rq
+or \(lqgo build *.go\(rq).
+Instead prefer to operate on complete packages (directories), such as:
+\(lqgo build .\(rq
+.PP
+Package names
+.PP
+Packages are identified by their import path.
+Import paths for packages in the standard library use their relative
+path under \(lq$GOROOT/src\(rq.
+Import paths for all other packages are a combination of their module
+name and their relative directory path within the module.
+Within a program, all packages must be identified by a unique import
+path.
+.PP
+Packages also have names, declared with the \(lqpackage\(rq keyword in a
+\&.go file, and used as the identifier when imported by another package.
+By convention, the names of importable packages match the last element
+of their import path, generally the name of the directory containing the
+package.
+.PP
+Package names do not have to be unique within a module, but packages
+that share the same name can\(cqt be imported together without one of
+them being aliased to a different name.
+.PP
+As the go command primarily operates on directories, all non test .go
+files within a directory (excluding subdirectories) should share the
+same package declaration.
+Test files may suffix their package declaration with \(lq_test\(rq,
+tests in these files are compiled as a separate package and don\(cqt
+have access to unexported identifiers of their corresponding package.
+See \(lqgo help test\(rq and \(lqgo help testflag\(rq for details.
+.PP
+There following package names have special meanings:
+.IP \(bu 2
+\(lqmain\(rq denotes the top\-level package in a stand\-alone
+executable.
+\(lqmain\(rq packages cannot be imported.
+.IP \(bu 2
+\(lqdocumentation\(rq indicates documentation for a non\-Go program in
+the directory.
+Files in package documentation are ignored by the go command.
+.IP \(bu 2
+\(lq_test\(rq suffix in \(lq*_test.go\(rq files.
+These form a separate test package that only has access to the colocated
+package\(cqs exported identifiers.
+See \(lqgo doc testing\(rq for details.
+.PP
+For more information about import paths, see \(lqgo help importpath\(rq.
+.SH AUTHOR
+This manual page was created using help2man and afterwards updating the
+output.
+It is maintained by the Debian Go Compiler Team
+<team+go\-compiler\(attracker.debian.org> for the Debian project (and
+may be used by others).
