+.TH "GO\-TOOL" "1" "August 2026" "Go 1.27" "Go programming tools and commands"
+.SH NAME
+go\-tool \- run specified go tool
+.SH SYNOPSIS
+\f[B]go tool\f[R] [\f[B]\-n\f[R]] \f[I]command\f[R]
+[\f[I]args\&...\f[R]]
+.SH DESCRIPTION
+Tool runs the go tool command identified by the arguments.
+.PP
+Go ships with a number of builtin tools, and additional tools may be
+defined in the go.mod of the current module.
+`go get \f[B]\-tool\f[R]' can be used to define additional tools in the
+current module\(cqs go.mod file.
+See `go help get' for more information.
+.PP
+The command can be specified using the full package path to the tool
+declared with a tool directive.
+The default binary name of the tool, which is the last component of the
+package path, excluding the major version suffix, can also be used if it
+is unique among declared tools.
+.PP
+With no arguments it prints the list of known tools.
+.SH OPTIONS
+The \f[B]\-n\f[R] flag causes tool to print the command that would be
+executed but not execute it.
+.PP
+The \f[B]\-modfile\f[R]=\f[I]file\f[R].mod build flag causes tool to use
+an alternate file instead of the go.mod in the module root directory.
+.PP
+Tool also provides the \f[B]\-C\f[R], \f[B]\-overlay\f[R], and
+\f[B]\-modcacherw\f[R] build flags.
+.PP
+The go command places \f[I]$GOROOT/bin\f[R] at the beginning of $PATH in
+the environment of commands run via tool directives, so that they use
+the same `go' as the parent `go tool'.
+.PP
+For more about build flags, see `go help build'.
+.PP
+For more about each builtin tool command, see `go doc cmd/<command>'.
